SPARTA Documentation
====================

The SPARTA manual is written in reStructuredText and built with Sphinx.
The sources are in src/; everything else in this directory is build
machinery. Generated output (html/, latex/, Manual.pdf, ...) is not
committed -- see .gitignore.

Building
--------

  make html      HTML manual in doc/html (start at doc/html/Manual.html)
  make pdf       Manual.pdf in this directory
  make epub      SPARTA.epub for e-book readers
  make fasthtml  quick approximate HTML preview, for editing (needs pandoc)
  make help      the full target list

The first build creates a Python virtual environment in doc/docenv and
installs the packages listed in utils/requirements.txt into it, so it
needs network access and takes a few minutes. Later builds reuse it.
"make clean-all" removes it.

Alternatively, cmake can drive the same build:

  cmake -S cmake -B build -DBUILD_DOC=ON
  cmake --build build --target doc

Checking
--------

  make check         run all of the checks below
  make anchor_check  scan for duplicate anchor labels
  make style_check   check that every style in src/ is documented
  make char_check    check for non-ASCII characters
  make role_check    check for misformatted reST roles
  make link_check    check external links (slow, needs network)
  make spelling      spell-check the manual (needs enchant)

Requirements
------------

  * python3 (3.8 or later) with the venv module
  * git, for cloning MathJax into the html tree so equations render
    without network access

For "make pdf", additionally:

  * pdflatex and latexmk
  * LaTeX packages: amsmath, anysize, babel, capt-of, cmap, dvipng,
    fncychap, framed, geometry, gyre, hyperref, hypcap, needspace,
    times, tabulary, titlesec, upquote, wrapfig

For "make epub": dvipng (equations are pre-rendered, since e-readers
cannot run MathJax).

For "make fasthtml": pandoc.

For "make spelling": the enchant library and an en_US dictionary.

Publishing
----------

The generated manual is not committed. Two mechanisms distribute it:

  * The website. sparta.github.io is a separate repository which serves
    the manual at https://sparta.github.io/doc/. It rebuilds the manual
    from these sources and commits the result into its own doc/
    directory. The workflow that does this is kept here as
    utils/website-publish.yml.template; copy it to
    .github/workflows/publish-manual.yml in the website repository. It
    needs no secrets.

  * Releases. .github/workflows/docs-release.yml builds the HTML and PDF
    manuals and attaches them to each GitHub release, so a release
    download still includes the standalone offline manual. Note that
    GitHub's auto-generated source tarballs contain only tracked files
    and therefore do not include it.

Every pull request builds the manual with warnings treated as errors and
runs the checks above, so a broken cross-reference fails CI.

Editing
-------

Doc pages are one .rst file per command, plus the Section_*.rst
chapters. Cross-reference other pages with :doc:`text <page>` and
labelled anchors with :ref:`text <label>`; both are validated at build
time, so a typo in a link fails the build rather than shipping a dead
link.

utils/converters/ holds the txt2html-to-reST converter used for the
one-time migration from the old doc/*.txt format. It is kept because
the rst_anchor_check tool ships in the same package, and is installed
into the virtual environment by "make html".
